{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>If his master gives him a wife.</b> We find that the speakers of the Holy Language show honor in the matter of names but not in verbs. They said אדון, also אדונים, and it is singular: ביד אדונים קשה (Isaiah 19:4). And with suffixes they said אדוניו and did not say אדונו, for it is not found. And in the first person אַדֹנִי שאל (Genesis 44:19), and it is forbidden to say it in plural, lest it intermingle with the honored Name. And similarly it is not fitting to say to Him in singular in the manner of the epithet אדני שאל (ibid.). And likewise the word אלוה — Rabbi Marinus said: זו כחו לאלהו (Habakkuk 1:11) and not לאלהיו, by way of disgrace:" "<b>If his master gives him a wife.</b> This wife, according to our Sages, is a Canaanite. And it is possible for the meaning of Canaanite to follow וגם מבני התושבים הגרים עמכם (Leviticus 25:45), concerning which is written מהם תקנו עבד ואמה (ibid. v. 44), and likewise every Canaanite slave. For concerning the seven nations it is written לא תחיה כל נשמה (Deuteronomy 20:16). And concerning Solomon it is written, מן הגוים אשר אמר ה' הם לא יבאו בכם, ואתם לא תבאו בהם (1 Kings 11:2), and this is written concerning Sidonian Hittite women, and not Moabite and Ammonite women:"], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "The meaning of the last clause is that אלהיו would imply a pronominal suffix attached to a divine name form, which is inappropriate in this derogatory context."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:36:43.728212723Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}